course: dictionary
lesson: 15605
title: Tangible: real enough to touch or measure
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Tangible is a word you can understand by thinking about touch. Something tangible is real, and you could touch it. «The gold coin was tangible: Maya could hold it in her hand.» You can feel a coin, so it is tangible.
explain: Tangible can also mean clear enough to be seen or measured. «The class made tangible progress: their spelling scores went up by ten marks.» You cannot touch progress, but you can count the marks, so the progress is tangible. Ask yourself: can I see it, measure it, or touch it?
explain: Tangible is often used with words like results, proof and benefits. «Ella wanted tangible proof that her plant was growing, so she measured it every day.» Proof you can measure is tangible. A good check: swap in «real and clear» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Tangible means real and able to be touched, like a tangible object.
fact: Tangible can also mean clear and definite enough to be seen or measured, like tangible results or tangible proof.
fact: «Tangible» is the opposite of vague: a vague feeling is hard to pin down, but tangible progress can be shown.
fact: Common phrases: «tangible evidence», «tangible results», «tangible benefits».
fact: Feelings are not usually tangible, but a feeling can be so strong that it seems almost «tangible».
exercise: «The gold coin was tangible: Maya could hold it in her hand.» What does tangible mean here? | *Real, and able to be touched | Loud and noisy | Very old | Invisible and far away
exercise: «The class made tangible progress: their spelling scores went up by ten marks.» What makes the progress tangible? | The teacher was in a good mood | *The scores went up, so you can see and measure it | The class felt happy about it | The room was tidy that day
exercise: «Ella wanted tangible proof that her plant was growing.» Find and copy the word that means clear enough to be seen or measured. | Ella | plant | wanted | *tangible
test: Tom says tangible means invisible. What is the right meaning? | Quiet and calm | *Real, and clear enough to be seen or measured | Invisible and hidden | Very old
test: Which of these is a tangible thing? | A rumour that no one can check | A happy feeling with no proof | A bad dream | *A brick wall
exam: «The new library brought tangible benefits: more children read every week.» What does tangible mean in this sentence? | Hidden under the floor | Full of noise | Only imaginary | *Clear and real, something you can see
